BT 1 0 0 1 0 149 Tm 14.5 TL /F1 10.5 Tf 0 0 0 rg (A small ) Tj /F2 10.5 Tf (Arduino Nano ESP32) Tj /F1 10.5 Tf ( \(45 x 18 mm, Bluetooth built in\) controls 20 lighting channels through three) Tj T* /F2 10.5 Tf (TBD62083APG driver chips) Tj /F1 10.5 Tf (: 7 engine channels with soft start, breathing and independent shimmer, 12) Tj T* (zones, and one accent channel. Each zone is a cluster of LEDs feeding one bundle of fibre optics: 10 hull) Tj T* -0.492537 Tw (zones of 5 LEDs, a bridge zone of 5 LEDs \(two for the bridge towers, three for the raised upper-hull section\) and) Tj T* 0 Tw -0.09499 Tw (a hangar bay zone of 5 LEDs. That is 67 LEDs in total, plus ) Tj /F2 10.5 Tf (6 SMD accent LEDs) Tj /F1 10.5 Tf ( \(three along each side of the) Tj T* 0 Tw -0.023377 Tw (hull\) on the 20th channel, which glow directly rather than through fibres. You switch everything on and off from) Tj T* 0 Tw (a phone with Adafruit's free ) Tj /F2 10.5 Tf (Bluefruit Connect) Tj /F1 10.5 Tf ( app - no custom app, and no separate Bluetooth module.) Tj T* /F2 10.5 Tf (Power requirement: a single 5 V USB-C charger) Tj /F1 10.5 Tf ( \(any phone charger capable of USB-C Power Delivery is) Tj T* (fine\), feeding a USB-C PD breakout board in the base. No mains wiring, battery pack or other power supply is) Tj T* (needed - the breakout board is what turns a standard charger into the clean 5 V rail the electronics run from.) Tj T* (When you press ON the lights come on in this order:) Tj T* ET
